From b0eb13a8e9bf7cdb4577db35a78f02d47a4d7a95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: songjiun1105 <tvpcqs5n2x@privaterelay.appleid.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 09:05:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Halo: make --halo-vibrancy-* self-contained and leak-free

Patch 1 created the blur/tint views inside UpdateGlassEligibility(), which
only runs when GlassFrameService exists (kGlassFrame feature, a local-state
pref, no battery saver, a single tracked window), and the views were
anonymous locals that were never stored or removed. This makes the switch
work on its own and bounds the views' lifetime:

- create the gutter from OnWidgetInitDone() so the switch alone is enough,
  store the blur/tint as members, and remove them in OnWidgetDestroyed()
  so eligibility cycles cannot stack copies;
- scope every consumer through halo::IsVibrancyEnabledForBrowser(): only
  the --app window is treated, a malformed --halo-vibrancy-width disables
  the treatment instead of leaving a chromeless transparent window, and
  popups/devtools in the same process keep upstream behavior;
- follow the text direction (leading edge in RTL);
- never show the glass-frame opt-in promo in the halo window;
- keep the window non-opaque when glass eligibility turns off;
- in ContentsWebView::UpdateBackgroundColor(), look the tab up only when
  web_contents() is set: the unguarded lookup crashed at startup before
  SetWebContents() ran.

Verified on out/TrialLean/Chromium.app at --window-size=1600,1000:
window.innerHeight is 1000 with the switch (full-size content view) against
968 without it; the leading strip tracks a red/blue/green backdrop behind
the window both with and without --enable-features=GlassFrame, while the
stock build with the same flags paints it opaque; the page area right of
the strip stays opaque #101010. The blur was also confirmed by eye.
